      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "From a legal standpoint, opponents often challenge the notion of continuous state liability, arguing that historical wrongs do not create unending present-day legal obligations. Philosophically, the debate often centers on whether responsibility should be collective—held by society as a whole—or individual, which complicates the claim when the harm was perpetrated by specific individuals long ago. Some argue that individual responsibility for past actions is diminished across generations, while others contend that systemic injustice requires a collective acknowledgment and remedy.",
      "source": "target",
